# Jira traps this tool works around

None of this is specific to one company — it is how Jira behaves. The concrete
values below were discovered from `${server}` at install time.

## The same status has two spellings

Jira renders status names through its localisation layer in the REST **fields**,
while the **changelog** keeps the underlying English names. So for one issue:

- `issue.fields.status.name` → the localised name
- `changelog.histories[].items[].toString` → the English name

Match on one spelling only and you lose every transition of that class **with no
error**. The remaining rows still look plausible, which is what makes it
dangerous: on one real 12-day window this silently dropped 50 of 129 selected
issues before it was caught.

`jira-flow-report init` recovers the mapping empirically instead of hardcoding a
table: an issue's **last** status transition and its **current** status are by
definition the same status, so pairing them across a few hundred issues yields
the map. It is stored in the snapshot, so the offline steps need no config.

