A changelog name that never appears as a last transition cannot be mapped this
way; it is reported under its changelog spelling rather than guessed at.

## Localised names are rejected in JQL

The instance can accept a localised name where the API returns it verbatim and
still reject it in a search. Two rules that follow:

- filter on `statusCategory` (`new` / `indeterminate` / `Done`) rather than
  `status` when you can — it sidesteps naming entirely;
- `issuetype` often needs the English name in JQL even when creation needs the
  localised one. The two directions catch people out equally.

This tool never puts a status name in JQL. It fetches by project and label and
does all status work client-side, which is why it is immune to this one.

## Labels are case-sensitive in JQL

`devops`, `devOps` and `DevOps` are three different labels to JQL, and real
projects accumulate more than one spelling. `collect` fetches every spelling
variant it was configured with; `aggregate` folds them case-insensitively by
default (`--no-fold-case` to keep them apart).

## A board filter is not the project

A board shows what its saved filter matches, and that filter usually hides old
resolved work — something like `resolved >= -14d OR resolution is EMPTY`. A
report that queries the project by label directly, which this tool does, sees
**more** than the board does.

That is normally what you want for a period report, but the counts will not match
what someone reads off the board by eye. Say which one you used.

